# Three-Tier Architecture on AWS

## What I Learned Today

Today I explored the **Three-Tier Architecture model on AWS** — one of the most commonly referenced architectures in AWS interviews and real-world projects. The session covers what the three tiers are, how AWS services map to each tier, the full request flow, and critically — when to mention this architecture in interviews and when not to.

---

## Application Architecture Tiers — Overview

| Tier | Layer | Description | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1-Tier** | Single layer | Frontend only — no backend, no database | Static HTML page, simple calculator UI |
| **2-Tier** | Frontend + Backend | No database required | Calculator with compute logic on backend |
| **3-Tier** | Frontend + Backend + Database | Full application stack | E-commerce site (amazon.com) |

### What Makes an Application "Three-Tier"?

Any application with all three of the following components follows the three-tier architecture:

1. **Frontend (Tier 1)** — User Interface (UI); what the user sees and interacts with in the browser.
2. **Backend (Tier 2)** — Application logic; processes requests from the frontend, written in Java, Python, Go, etc.
3. **Database (Tier 3)** — Stores and retrieves data; the backend queries the database and returns results to the frontend.

### Request Flow Example (amazon.com)

```
User opens browser → searches amazon.com
         ↓
Frontend (HTML/UI) — user logs in, clicks product
         ↓
Backend — processes the request, authenticates to database
         ↓
Database — returns product details
         ↓
Backend — formats response
         ↓
Frontend — displays product information to user
```

---

## Three-Tier Architecture on AWS — Components

### VPC and Subnet Design

The entire architecture lives inside a **Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)** — isolated from other projects/teams in the same AWS account.

```
VPC (e.g., 172.16.0.0/16)
├── Public Subnet          — Load Balancer (internet-facing)
├── Private Subnet 1       — Frontend EC2 instances (Auto Scaling Group)
├── Private Subnet 2       — Backend EC2 instances (Auto Scaling Group)
└── Database Subnet        — RDS (Primary + Secondary)
```

Each subnet type serves a specific tier and is kept isolated for security — users cannot reach the backend directly; the backend cannot be accessed without going through the frontend load balancer.

---

### Full Architecture Diagram

```
User
  ↓
Route 53 (DNS)
  ↓
CloudFront (CDN — optional, for caching)
  ↓
Application Load Balancer (Public Subnet — internet-facing)
  ↓
Frontend EC2 Instances — Auto Scaling Group
  (Private Subnet — us-east-1a, us-east-1b)
  ↓
Application Load Balancer (Internal — private)
  ↓
Backend EC2 Instances — Auto Scaling Group
  (Private Subnet — us-east-1a, us-east-1b)
  ↓
Amazon RDS — Primary Instance
  ↓ (replication)
Amazon RDS — Secondary Instance (standby for failover)
  (Database Subnet)
```

---

## AWS Services Used at Each Tier

| Layer | AWS Service | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| DNS | **Route 53** | Resolves domain to load balancer |
| CDN | **CloudFront** | Caches static content at edge locations |
| Public entry point | **Application Load Balancer** | Distributes traffic to frontend instances |
| Frontend compute | **EC2 + Auto Scaling Group** | Hosts frontend app; scales across 2 AZs |
| Internal routing | **Application Load Balancer (internal)** | Routes frontend → backend |
| Backend compute | **EC2 + Auto Scaling Group** | Hosts backend app; scales across 2 AZs |
| Database | **Amazon RDS** | Managed relational database (MySQL, PostgreSQL) |
| High availability | **RDS Multi-AZ** | Primary + Secondary RDS for failover |
| Isolation | **VPC + Subnets** | Separates tiers into private network segments |

---

## Why Auto Scaling Groups?

- Auto Scaling Groups deploy EC2 instances across **multiple Availability Zones** (e.g., us-east-1a and us-east-1b).
- If one AZ goes down, instances in the other AZ continue serving traffic.
- Traffic spikes trigger **scale-out** (add instances); quiet periods trigger **scale-in** (remove instances).
- This ensures **high availability** and **cost efficiency** simultaneously.

---

## Why Primary + Secondary RDS?

- The **Primary RDS** handles all read/write operations.
- The **Secondary RDS** (standby) is a replica — automatically promoted if the primary fails.
- This prevents **data loss** and reduces **downtime** during database failures.
- In AWS, this is called **RDS Multi-AZ deployment**.

---

## Disaster Recovery — Multi-Region Setup

For critical applications, the entire three-tier architecture can be replicated in a **second AWS region**:

```
Region 1 (us-east-1)                Region 2 (eu-west-1)
┌─────────────────────┐             ┌─────────────────────┐
│  VPC + Full Stack   │  ←replication→ │  VPC + Full Stack   │
│  (Primary)          │             │  (Disaster Recovery) │
└─────────────────────┘             └─────────────────────┘
```

- If Region 1 becomes completely unavailable, Route 53 can automatically route traffic to Region 2.
- VPC Peering or AWS Transit Gateway connects the two regions.

---

## Security Considerations

- **Public Subnet** — Only the internet-facing load balancer lives here. EC2 instances are never directly exposed to the internet.
- **Private Subnets** — Frontend and backend EC2 instances are not publicly accessible. They can only receive traffic from the load balancer.
- **Database Subnet** — RDS instances have no internet connectivity. Only backend EC2 instances in the private subnet can access the database.
- **Security Groups** — Layer-by-layer access control: LB → frontend SG → backend SG → database SG.
- **IAM Roles** — EC2 instances use IAM roles to access other AWS services (e.g., S3, Secrets Manager) without hardcoded credentials.

---

## Interview Guidance — When to Use This Architecture

### ✅ Mention Three-Tier Architecture When:
- The job description mentions **EC2**, traditional web application hosting, or MNC/enterprise environments.
- The company is **not yet containerized** and still runs workloads on virtual machines.
- You are asked about architecting a **scalable web application** on AWS.

### ❌ Do NOT Lead with Three-Tier (EC2-based) When:
- The job description emphasizes **Kubernetes, EKS, containers, or microservices**.
- The company is clearly cloud-native or uses modern DevOps tooling.
- In these cases, explain the **EKS-based architecture** instead — containerized microservices with Kubernetes, Ingress controllers, and managed services.

> **Key principle:** Match the architecture you describe to what the company actually uses. Describing an EC2-based three-tier architecture to a Kubernetes-heavy company signals a lack of modern cloud-native experience.

---

## Three-Tier vs Kubernetes Architecture

| Aspect | Three-Tier (EC2) | Kubernetes (EKS) |
|---|---|---|
| Compute unit | EC2 instance | Container / Pod |
| Scaling | Auto Scaling Group | HPA / Cluster Autoscaler |
| Routing | Application Load Balancer | Ingress Controller |
| Service discovery | DNS / Load Balancer | Kubernetes Service |
| Deployment | AMI + Launch Template | Docker image + Helm/kubectl |
| Best for | Traditional workloads, MNCs | Modern microservices, cloud-native |

